1. Time Complexity:
Time complexity is a measure of the amount of time an algorithm takes to complete based on the size of the
input. It helps estimate how the algorithm's performance will scale as the input size grows.

Example: Let's say you want to find a name in a list of names.
If the list has 10 names, and you look through each one, it will take about 10 steps.
If the list has 100 names, it will take about 100 steps.
The goal is to understand how the number of steps (time) increases as the size of the input (list of names)
grows.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Space Complexity:
Space complexity refers to how much extra memory (space) an algorithm needs as the input size increases.
This includes memory for variables, data structures, etc.

Example: If an algorithm creates an extra list to store the results, the space complexity depends on how big
that list will grow as input increases
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Big O Notation:
Big O notation is a way to describe the time or space complexity of an algorithm in a simplified manner.
It focuses on the worst-case scenario and gives an idea of the algorithm’s efficiency.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Common Big O Terms:

O(1) – Constant time: The time does not change with input size.
Example: Accessing an element in a list by index.

O(n) – Linear time: The time increases directly with the input size.
Example: Searching through an unsorted list.

O(n²) – Quadratic time: The time increases with the square of the input size.
Example: Comparing all pairs in a list.

O(log n) – Logarithmic time: The time grows slower as the input size increases.
Example: Binary search in a sorted list.

Simplified Example of Time Complexity:
O(1): You have a box. Opening the box takes the same amount of time, no matter how many items are inside.
O(n): You have a row of boxes. You open each one, so the more boxes there are, the longer it takes.
O(n²): You have rows of boxes, and you need to check every pair of boxes. The time grows quickly as the
number of boxes increases.

Simplified Example of Space Complexity:
O(1): You store one extra item regardless of how many items you are processing.
O(n): You need an extra list that grows as big as your input list.
